WHATNOW

What tam-edit is doing now and what comes next. Read this first; revise
it at the end of every session (work/README-work).

Updated: 2026-11-25, with W8


STATE

  tam-edit is a C11 editor that reproduces micro and links only libtam
  and the C library. The repository started fresh on 2026-11-18 (W3)
  with one commit holding the C11 editor at 1.0.4, on branch dev, and
  now follows the tam-idol 0.2.0 standard (W4). The old history (the
  fork of micro, the Go version on master, branches c11 and test) is
  archived read-only at greenermoose/tam-edit-micro-fork. Commit hashes
  in older records, R1-cursor-movement.md included, are in that
  archive. VERSION reads 1.1.0 (the footer shows it).

  All five migration milestones are complete (ROADMAP.md, 1.0.0 on
  2026-11-13): editing, views and configuration, syntax highlighting,
  search, encodings, shell, multi-cursor, the parity audit
  (docs/PARITY_AUDIT.md), fuzzing and packaging. Since then: fixes to
  arrow keys, softwrap boundaries, the softwrap goal column, the version
  in the footer, DeleteWordLeft (W2, 61cc02c0), R1 #2/#4/#5 (W5), and
  R1 #7/#6/#8 (W6).

  All eight findings of reviews/R1-cursor-movement.md are now fixed:
  #1 (softwrap goal column, 880cbe4e), #2 (visual column tabsize, W5),
  #3 (act_delete_word_left, 61cc02c0), #4 (tab-indent byte-vs-rune
  indexing, W5), #5 (empty-doc cursor_clamp, W5), #6 (extract scroll
  clamping from render.c, W6), #7 (multi-cursor downstream metadata,
  W6), and #8 (Unicode-aware word classes, W6).


  W7 adopts the Tamlinux default key map, named action palette, and
  Linux console fallbacks. The editor keeps the existing INI binding
  format. See keys/keybindings.md (moved from arch/keys.md in W8).

